\subsection{The Handshake}

Before any messages can be exchanged, peers must complete a handshake. Unlike
the length-prefixed messages that follow, the handshake has a fixed 68-byte
format defined by BEP 3. This asymmetry exists because the handshake predates
the message framing---it establishes identity before the protocol proper begins.

\subsubsection{Handshake Wire Format}

The handshake consists of five fields, transmitted in order:

\begin{verbatim}
+--------+---------------------+----------+-----------+---------+
| pstrlen|       pstr          | reserved | info_hash | peer_id |
+--------+---------------------+----------+-----------+---------+
| 1 byte |      19 bytes       | 8 bytes  | 20 bytes  | 20 bytes|
+--------+---------------------+----------+-----------+---------+
| = 19   | "BitTorrent protocol"| zeros   | SHA1 hash | client  |
+--------+---------------------+----------+-----------+---------+
                        Total: 68 bytes
\end{verbatim}

\begin{description}
\item[pstrlen (1 byte)] The length of the protocol string. For BitTorrent v1,
  this is always 19. Future protocols might use different lengths, so this
  field allows version detection.

\item[pstr (19 bytes)] The protocol identifier string: ``BitTorrent protocol''
  (ASCII, no null terminator). This confirms both peers speak the same protocol.

\item[reserved (8 bytes)] Eight bytes reserved for future extensions. BEP 3
  specifies these should be zeros. Extensions like BEP 10 (Extension Protocol)
  set specific bits here to advertise capabilities. For our basic implementation,
  we send and expect zeros.

\item[info\_hash (20 bytes)] The SHA1 hash of the bencoded info dictionary from
  the torrent metainfo. This identifies which torrent we want to exchange. Both
  peers must send the same info\_hash; a mismatch means they're talking about
  different torrents.

\item[peer\_id (20 bytes)] A unique identifier for this client instance. By
  convention, the first 8 bytes encode the client name and version (e.g.,
  ``-LB0001-'' for ``Literate BitTorrent version 0.0.01''), and the remaining
  12 bytes are random. This helps with debugging and statistics.
\end{description}

\subsubsection{Handshake Constants}

We define constants for the protocol string and total handshake length. Using
constants prevents magic numbers in the code and makes the protocol specification
explicit.

<<handshake constants>>=
/// The protocol identifier string for BitTorrent v1.
///
/// This 19-byte ASCII string is sent at the start of every handshake,
/// preceded by a single byte containing its length (19). The string
/// identifies the protocol version and must match exactly for peers
/// to communicate.
///
/// BEP 3 specifies this exact string. Alternative protocols (like
/// BitTorrent v2) would use different strings.
pub const PROTOCOL_STRING: &[u8; 19] = b"BitTorrent protocol";

/// The total length of a BitTorrent handshake in bytes.
///
/// The handshake is exactly 68 bytes:
/// - 1 byte: protocol string length (19)
/// - 19 bytes: protocol string ("BitTorrent protocol")
/// - 8 bytes: reserved (zeros for basic protocol)
/// - 20 bytes: info_hash (SHA1 of torrent's info dictionary)
/// - 20 bytes: peer_id (client identifier)
///
/// This constant is useful for pre-allocating buffers and validating
/// that we've received a complete handshake.
pub const HANDSHAKE_LENGTH: usize = 1 + 19 + 8 + 20 + 20; // = 68
@

Note that [[PROTOCOL_STRING]] is typed as [[&[u8; 19]]] rather than [[&str]].
While the content is ASCII text, we work with bytes throughout the wire protocol
to avoid any UTF-8 validation overhead and to match the binary nature of the
protocol.

\subsubsection{The Handshake Struct}

The [[Handshake]] struct captures the two variable parts of the handshake: the
info\_hash and peer\_id. The protocol string and reserved bytes are fixed, so
we don't store them---they're generated during serialization.

<<handshake struct>>=
/// A BitTorrent protocol handshake.
///
/// The handshake is exchanged immediately after TCP connection, before any
/// length-prefixed messages. It identifies both the torrent (via `info_hash`)
/// and the client (via `peer_id`).
///
/// # Wire Format
///
/// When serialized, a handshake is exactly 68 bytes:
/// ```text
/// <pstrlen><pstr><reserved><info_hash><peer_id>
///    1      19       8        20        20     = 68 bytes
/// ```
///
/// The `pstrlen` is always 19, `pstr` is always "BitTorrent protocol",
/// and `reserved` is 8 zero bytes (no extensions). Only `info_hash` and
/// `peer_id` vary between handshakes.
///
/// # Example
///
/// ```
/// use literate_bittorrent::messages::Handshake;
///
/// let info_hash = [0u8; 20]; // In practice, SHA1 of info dict
/// let peer_id = *b"-LB0001-AABBCCDDEEFF"; // Client identifier
///
/// let handshake = Handshake::new(info_hash, peer_id);
/// assert_eq!(handshake.info_hash(), &info_hash);
/// assert_eq!(handshake.peer_id(), &peer_id);
/// ```
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub struct Handshake {
    /// The SHA1 hash of the torrent's bencoded info dictionary.
    ///
    /// This 20-byte value uniquely identifies the torrent. Both peers in a
    /// connection must have the same info_hash, or they're talking about
    /// different torrents.
    info_hash: [u8; 20],

    /// The unique identifier for this client instance.
    ///
    /// By convention, the first 8 bytes encode client name and version
    /// (e.g., "-LB0001-" for "Literate BitTorrent v0.0.01"), and the
    /// remaining 12 bytes are random to ensure uniqueness.
    peer_id: [u8; 20],
}
@

We derive [[Clone]] and [[PartialEq]]/[[Eq]] since handshakes are small value
types that we might need to copy or compare. The [[Debug]] implementation is
useful for logging during connection debugging.

\subsubsection{Handshake Constructor and Accessors}

The constructor is straightforward since both fields are fixed-size arrays.
The inputs must already be the correct size, enforced by Rust's type system.

<<handshake impl>>=
impl Handshake {
    /// Creates a new handshake with the given info hash and peer ID.
    ///
    /// Both arguments are 20-byte arrays, matching the wire protocol
    /// specification. The type system ensures correct sizes at compile time.
    ///
    /// # Arguments
    ///
    /// * `info_hash` - SHA1 hash of the torrent's bencoded info dictionary
    /// * `peer_id` - 20-byte client identifier (typically 8-byte prefix + 12 random bytes)
    ///
    /// # Example
    ///
    /// ```
    /// use literate_bittorrent::messages::Handshake;
    ///
    /// // Create a handshake for a torrent
    /// let info_hash = [0xAB; 20]; // Would be actual SHA1 in practice
    /// let peer_id = *b"-LB0001-123456789012";
    ///
    /// let hs = Handshake::new(info_hash, peer_id);
    /// ```
    #[must_use]
    pub fn new(info_hash: [u8; 20], peer_id: [u8; 20]) -> Self {
        Self { info_hash, peer_id }
    }

    /// Returns the info hash from this handshake.
    ///
    /// The info hash identifies which torrent this connection is for.
    /// After receiving a peer's handshake, compare this to your expected
    /// info hash to verify you're both talking about the same torrent.
    #[must_use]
    pub fn info_hash(&self) -> &[u8; 20] {
        &self.info_hash
    }

    /// Returns the peer ID from this handshake.
    ///
    /// The peer ID identifies the remote client. Useful for logging,
    /// debugging, and potentially for peer statistics.
    #[must_use]
    pub fn peer_id(&self) -> &[u8; 20] {
        &self.peer_id
    }

    /// Returns the reserved bytes for this handshake.
    ///
    /// In the basic BitTorrent protocol, reserved bytes are all zeros.
    /// Extensions like BEP 10 would set specific bits here. For now,
    /// we always return zeros.
    #[must_use]
    pub fn reserved(&self) -> [u8; 8] {
        [0u8; 8]
    }

    <<handshake to bytes>>

    <<handshake from bytes>>

    <<handshake validate info hash>>
}
@

The [[#[must_use]]] attribute warns if the return value is discarded, which
would indicate a likely bug (why create a handshake and ignore its fields?).

Note that [[new]] takes ownership of the arrays rather than references. Since
[[u8; 20]]] is [[Copy]], there's no performance difference, and taking ownership
is clearer---the handshake owns its data.

\subsubsection{Handshake Serialization}

To transmit a handshake over the wire, we must serialize it to exactly 68 bytes.
Unlike the length-prefixed messages that follow, the handshake has no length
field---the receiver must read exactly 68 bytes and validate them.

The serialization is straightforward: concatenate the five fields in order.
We use a fixed-size array [[u8; 68]]] rather than a [[Vec<u8>]] because:

\begin{itemize}
\item The size is known at compile time, eliminating heap allocation
\item The type system enforces correctness---we cannot accidentally produce
  a buffer of the wrong size
\item Fixed-size arrays are efficient to copy and can be passed by value
\end{itemize}

<<handshake to bytes>>=
/// Serializes this handshake to its 68-byte wire format.
///
/// The returned array contains:
/// - Byte 0: protocol string length (19)
/// - Bytes 1-19: protocol string ("BitTorrent protocol")
/// - Bytes 20-27: reserved bytes (8 zeros)
/// - Bytes 28-47: info_hash (20 bytes)
/// - Bytes 48-67: peer_id (20 bytes)
///
/// This format matches BEP 3 exactly. The handshake can be written directly
/// to a TCP socket.
///
/// # Example
///
/// ```
/// use literate_bittorrent::messages::Handshake;
///
/// let info_hash = [0xAB; 20];
/// let peer_id = *b"-LB0001-123456789012";
/// let hs = Handshake::new(info_hash, peer_id);
///
/// let bytes = hs.to_bytes();
/// assert_eq!(bytes.len(), 68);
/// assert_eq!(bytes[0], 19); // pstrlen
/// assert_eq!(&bytes[1..20], b"BitTorrent protocol");
/// assert_eq!(&bytes[28..48], &info_hash);
/// assert_eq!(&bytes[48..68], &peer_id);
/// ```
#[must_use]
pub fn to_bytes(&self) -> [u8; HANDSHAKE_LENGTH] {
    let mut buf = [0u8; HANDSHAKE_LENGTH];

    // Byte 0: protocol string length
    buf[0] = 19;

    // Bytes 1-19: protocol string
    buf[1..20].copy_from_slice(PROTOCOL_STRING);

    // Bytes 20-27: reserved (already zeros from initialization)
    // No action needed since we initialized with zeros

    // Bytes 28-47: info_hash
    buf[28..48].copy_from_slice(&self.info_hash);

    // Bytes 48-67: peer_id
    buf[48..68].copy_from_slice(&self.peer_id);

    buf
}
@

The implementation uses array slicing with [[copy_from_slice]], which is both
safe and efficient. The compiler can verify that our slice ranges match the
source array sizes, catching off-by-one errors at compile time.

Note that we initialize the buffer with zeros, which automatically handles
the reserved bytes (positions 20-27). This is a common pattern: zero-initialize
and then fill in the non-zero parts. If we ever support protocol extensions
that use the reserved bytes, we would add logic here to set specific bits.

\subsubsection{Handshake Parsing (Deserialization)}

When receiving a handshake from a peer, we must parse the 68-byte buffer and
validate its contents. Unlike construction (where Rust's type system enforces
correctness), parsing untrusted network data requires explicit validation at
every step.

The parsing process is the inverse of serialization, but with an important
asymmetry: serialization cannot fail (we control the input), while parsing
can fail in many ways (the peer controls the input). We return [[Result]] to
propagate validation failures, using [[PeerError::HandshakeFailed]] with
descriptive messages.

The validation order matters for security and performance:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Length check}: Reject immediately if not exactly 68 bytes. This
  prevents out-of-bounds reads and provides a clear error for truncated data.

\item \textbf{Protocol string length}: The first byte must be 19. A different
  value indicates either a different protocol version (unlikely) or corrupted
  data. We check this before reading the protocol string to fail fast.

\item \textbf{Protocol string}: The 19 bytes starting at position 1 must match
  ``BitTorrent protocol'' exactly. This confirms the peer speaks our protocol.

\item \textbf{Reserved bytes}: We read but do not validate the 8 reserved bytes.
  Extensions set bits here; for forward compatibility we accept any values.

\item \textbf{Info hash and peer ID}: Extract the final 40 bytes. These are
  opaque binary data with no intrinsic validity constraints.
\end{enumerate}

Note that we do \emph{not} validate the info\_hash during parsing. The parser
extracts whatever 20 bytes are present; the caller must compare against the
expected info\_hash using [[validate_info_hash]]. This separation keeps parsing
pure (no external context needed) and makes the security-critical check explicit.

<<handshake from bytes>>=
/// Parses a handshake from its 68-byte wire format.
///
/// This method validates the handshake structure according to BEP 3:
/// - Input must be exactly 68 bytes
/// - First byte (pstrlen) must be 19
/// - Bytes 1-19 must be "BitTorrent protocol"
///
/// The reserved bytes (20-27) are accepted but not validated, allowing
/// forward compatibility with protocol extensions.
///
/// # Arguments
///
/// * `bytes` - A byte slice containing the handshake data
///
/// # Returns
///
/// * `Ok(Handshake)` - Successfully parsed handshake
/// * `Err(PeerError::HandshakeFailed)` - Validation failed
///
/// # Errors
///
/// Returns [`PeerError::HandshakeFailed`] if:
/// - Input length is not 68 bytes
/// - Protocol string length byte is not 19
/// - Protocol string is not "BitTorrent protocol"
///
/// # Example
///
/// ```
/// use literate_bittorrent::messages::{Handshake, PeerError, HANDSHAKE_LENGTH};
///
/// // Create a valid handshake buffer
/// let info_hash = [0xAB; 20];
/// let peer_id = *b"-LB0001-123456789012";
/// let original = Handshake::new(info_hash, peer_id);
/// let bytes = original.to_bytes();
///
/// // Parse it back
/// let parsed = Handshake::from_bytes(&bytes).unwrap();
/// assert_eq!(parsed.info_hash(), &info_hash);
/// assert_eq!(parsed.peer_id(), &peer_id);
///
/// // Invalid handshakes are rejected
/// let short_buffer = [0u8; 50];
/// assert!(Handshake::from_bytes(&short_buffer).is_err());
/// ```
pub fn from_bytes(bytes: &[u8]) -> Result<Self, PeerError> {
    // Validate length first - this is the fastest check and prevents
    // out-of-bounds access in subsequent steps
    if bytes.len() != HANDSHAKE_LENGTH {
        return Err(PeerError::HandshakeFailed(format!(
            "expected {} bytes, got {}",
            HANDSHAKE_LENGTH,
            bytes.len()
        )));
    }

    // Validate protocol string length (pstrlen)
    let pstrlen = bytes[0];
    if pstrlen != 19 {
        return Err(PeerError::HandshakeFailed(format!(
            "invalid protocol string length: expected 19, got {}",
            pstrlen
        )));
    }

    // Validate protocol string (pstr)
    let pstr = &bytes[1..20];
    if pstr != PROTOCOL_STRING {
        // Include the actual string if it's valid UTF-8, otherwise show hex
        let pstr_display = match std::str::from_utf8(pstr) {
            Ok(s) => format!("\"{}\"", s),
            Err(_) => format!("{:02x?}", pstr),
        };
        return Err(PeerError::HandshakeFailed(format!(
            "invalid protocol string: expected \"BitTorrent protocol\", got {}",
            pstr_display
        )));
    }

    // Reserved bytes (20-27) are accepted without validation for
    // forward compatibility with extensions like BEP 10

    // Extract info_hash (bytes 28-47)
    let info_hash: [u8; 20] = bytes[28..48]
        .try_into()
        .expect("slice length verified above");

    // Extract peer_id (bytes 48-67)
    let peer_id: [u8; 20] = bytes[48..68]
        .try_into()
        .expect("slice length verified above");

    Ok(Self { info_hash, peer_id })
}
@

The [[try_into()]] calls convert slices to fixed-size arrays. We use [[expect]]
rather than [[?]] because the slice lengths are guaranteed by our prior length
check---if these panicked, it would indicate a bug in our code, not invalid input.

\subsubsection{Info Hash Validation}

After parsing a peer's handshake, the caller must verify the info\_hash matches
their expected torrent. This is a critical security check: without it, a malicious
peer could inject data for the wrong torrent.

We provide a dedicated method for this check rather than requiring callers to
do the comparison themselves. This makes the security-critical operation explicit
and self-documenting, and allows for future enhancements (like constant-time
comparison if we ever handle sensitive hashes).

<<handshake validate info hash>>=
/// Validates that this handshake's info_hash matches the expected value.
///
/// After receiving a peer's handshake, you **must** call this method to
/// verify the peer is serving the torrent you requested. Skipping this
/// check is a security vulnerability: the peer could send data for a
/// completely different torrent.
///
/// # Arguments
///
/// * `expected` - The info_hash of the torrent you are downloading
///
/// # Returns
///
/// * `Ok(())` - The info hashes match
/// * `Err(PeerError::HandshakeFailed)` - The info hashes do not match
///
/// # Example
///
/// ```
/// use literate_bittorrent::messages::Handshake;
///
/// let expected_hash = [0xAB; 20];
/// let handshake = Handshake::new([0xAB; 20], *b"-PEER-ID-12345678901");
///
/// // Validation succeeds when hashes match
/// assert!(handshake.validate_info_hash(&expected_hash).is_ok());
///
/// // Validation fails when hashes differ
/// let wrong_hash = [0xCD; 20];
/// assert!(handshake.validate_info_hash(&wrong_hash).is_err());
/// ```
pub fn validate_info_hash(&self, expected: &[u8; 20]) -> Result<(), PeerError> {
    if &self.info_hash == expected {
        Ok(())
    } else {
        Err(PeerError::HandshakeFailed(format!(
            "info_hash mismatch: expected {:02x?}, got {:02x?}",
            expected, self.info_hash
        )))
    }
}
@

The error message includes both the expected and actual hashes in hexadecimal
format, which is invaluable for debugging. In production, you might truncate
these to avoid log spam, but for development the full hashes help diagnose
configuration issues (e.g., using the wrong torrent file).

