\subsection{Peer Wire Protocol Messages}

After completing the handshake, peers communicate using length-prefixed messages.
Each message (except keep-alive) has a one-byte type identifier followed by a
payload whose structure depends on the message type. The BitTorrent protocol
defines nine message types with IDs 0--8, plus a special keep-alive message
that has no ID (it's just a zero-length message).

\subsubsection{Message ID Constants}

BEP 3 assigns specific numeric IDs to each message type. We define these as
constants to avoid magic numbers throughout the codebase and to document the
protocol specification clearly.

<<message id constants>>=
/// Message ID for the `choke` message.
///
/// A peer sends choke (ID 0) to indicate it will not fulfill any pending
/// requests from the receiver. The receiver should not send any more
/// requests until it receives an unchoke. Choking is the primary mechanism
/// for upload bandwidth management in BitTorrent.
pub const MSG_CHOKE: u8 = 0;

/// Message ID for the `unchoke` message.
///
/// A peer sends unchoke (ID 1) to indicate it is now willing to fulfill
/// requests from the receiver. After receiving unchoke, a peer may begin
/// sending request messages for blocks.
pub const MSG_UNCHOKE: u8 = 1;

/// Message ID for the `interested` message.
///
/// A peer sends interested (ID 2) to indicate it wants pieces that the
/// remote peer has. This is used by the remote peer's choking algorithm
/// to decide whom to unchoke. A peer should send interested when it sees
/// the remote has pieces it needs, and not interested when the remote has
/// nothing it wants.
pub const MSG_INTERESTED: u8 = 2;

/// Message ID for the `not interested` message.
///
/// A peer sends not interested (ID 3) to indicate it does not want any
/// pieces the remote peer currently has. This helps the remote peer
/// prioritize connections with peers that actually need data.
pub const MSG_NOT_INTERESTED: u8 = 3;

/// Message ID for the `have` message.
///
/// A peer sends have (ID 4) after successfully downloading and verifying
/// a piece to announce that it now has that piece available. The payload
/// is the 4-byte big-endian piece index.
pub const MSG_HAVE: u8 = 4;

/// Message ID for the `bitfield` message.
///
/// A peer sends bitfield (ID 5) immediately after the handshake (and
/// before any other messages) to indicate which pieces it has. The
/// payload is a bitfield where bit i (from the high bit of byte 0) is
/// set if the peer has piece i. The bitfield must be the correct length
/// for the torrent (ceil(num_pieces / 8) bytes), with spare bits at the
/// end set to zero.
pub const MSG_BITFIELD: u8 = 5;

/// Message ID for the `request` message.
///
/// A peer sends request (ID 6) to ask for a block of data. The payload
/// is 12 bytes: piece index (4 bytes), byte offset within piece (4 bytes),
/// and block length (4 bytes). All values are big-endian unsigned integers.
/// The typical block size is 16KB (2^14 bytes).
pub const MSG_REQUEST: u8 = 6;

/// Message ID for the `piece` message.
///
/// A peer sends piece (ID 7) in response to a request, delivering the
/// actual data. The payload is: piece index (4 bytes), byte offset (4
/// bytes), and the block data (variable length, typically 16KB). This
/// is the only message that transfers actual file content.
pub const MSG_PIECE: u8 = 7;

/// Message ID for the `cancel` message.
///
/// A peer sends cancel (ID 8) to cancel a previously sent request. The
/// payload format is identical to request: piece index, byte offset,
/// and length. Cancellation is useful during endgame mode when the same
/// block may be requested from multiple peers.
pub const MSG_CANCEL: u8 = 8;

/// The standard block size for BitTorrent data transfer.
///
/// By convention, peers request data in 16KB (2^14 = 16384 byte) blocks.
/// This is not mandated by the protocol but is a de facto standard that
/// all implementations respect. Using a different block size might cause
/// compatibility issues with some peers.
///
/// The last block in a piece may be smaller than this if the piece length
/// is not a multiple of BLOCK_SIZE.
pub const BLOCK_SIZE: u32 = 16384; // 2^14 = 16 KiB
@

Note that keep-alive has no message ID constant because it has no ID byte at
all---it's simply a message with length zero. The message ID constants use a
[[MSG_]] prefix to clearly identify them as message type identifiers.

\subsubsection{The Message Enum}

We represent all peer wire protocol messages as variants of a single enum.
This provides several benefits:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Type safety}: The compiler ensures all message types are handled
  in match expressions, preventing accidental omissions.

\item \textbf{Clear payload structure}: Each variant's fields document exactly
  what data that message type carries.

\item \textbf{Ergonomic pattern matching}: Message handlers can destructure
  messages directly in match arms.
\end{itemize}

<<message enum>>=
/// A peer wire protocol message.
///
/// This enum represents all message types defined in BEP 3. After the initial
/// handshake, all peer communication uses these length-prefixed messages.
///
/// # Wire Format
///
/// Messages are transmitted as: `<length><id><payload>` where:
/// - `length` is a 4-byte big-endian integer (total bytes after length field)
/// - `id` is a 1-byte message type identifier (omitted for keep-alive)
/// - `payload` varies by message type (may be empty)
///
/// # Keep-Alive
///
/// The `KeepAlive` variant represents a zero-length message (no ID, no payload).
/// Peers should send keep-alives every 2 minutes to prevent timeout disconnection.
///
/// # Example
///
/// ```
/// use literate_bittorrent::messages::{Message, MSG_CHOKE, MSG_HAVE};
///
/// // Create messages
/// let keepalive = Message::KeepAlive;
/// let choke = Message::Choke;
/// let have = Message::Have(42);
///
/// // Get message IDs
/// assert_eq!(keepalive.id(), None);  // Keep-alive has no ID
/// assert_eq!(choke.id(), Some(MSG_CHOKE));
/// assert_eq!(have.id(), Some(MSG_HAVE));
/// ```
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum Message {
    /// Keep-alive message (length = 0, no ID).
    ///
    /// Peers should send keep-alive messages at least once every 2 minutes
    /// to prevent the connection from being closed due to inactivity.
    /// Keep-alives have no payload and no message ID---they're just a
    /// 4-byte length prefix of zero.
    KeepAlive,

    /// Choke message (ID = 0, no payload).
    ///
    /// Indicates this peer will not respond to requests from the remote peer.
    /// When choked, the remote peer should stop sending requests until it
    /// receives an `Unchoke` message. Pending requests should be considered
    /// discarded.
    Choke,

    /// Unchoke message (ID = 1, no payload).
    ///
    /// Indicates this peer is now willing to respond to requests. The remote
    /// peer may begin sending `Request` messages after receiving `Unchoke`.
    Unchoke,

    /// Interested message (ID = 2, no payload).
    ///
    /// Indicates this peer is interested in pieces the remote peer has.
    /// A peer should send `Interested` when it discovers the remote peer
    /// has pieces it needs, and `NotInterested` otherwise. This information
    /// helps peers decide whom to unchoke.
    Interested,

    /// Not Interested message (ID = 3, no payload).
    ///
    /// Indicates this peer is not interested in any pieces the remote peer
    /// currently has. This may change as the remote peer acquires new pieces
    /// or as this peer's needs change.
    NotInterested,

    /// Have message (ID = 4, payload = piece index).
    ///
    /// Announces that this peer has successfully downloaded and verified
    /// the piece at the given index. Peers send `Have` after completing a
    /// piece to update the swarm about their availability.
    ///
    /// The contained value is the zero-based piece index.
    Have(u32),

    /// Bitfield message (ID = 5, payload = bitfield).
    ///
    /// Sent immediately after the handshake (before any other messages) to
    /// indicate which pieces this peer has. Bit i (counting from the high
    /// bit of byte 0) is set if piece i is available.
    ///
    /// The bitfield must have the correct length: `ceil(num_pieces / 8)` bytes.
    /// Any spare bits at the end must be zero.
    ///
    /// A peer that has no pieces may either send an empty bitfield or skip
    /// the bitfield message entirely.
    Bitfield(Vec<u8>),

    /// Request message (ID = 6, payload = index + begin + length).
    ///
    /// Requests a block of data from a piece. The block is identified by:
    /// - `index`: the piece index (0-based)
    /// - `begin`: byte offset within the piece
    /// - `length`: number of bytes requested (typically 16KB)
    ///
    /// Requests should only be sent when not choked by the remote peer.
    /// The remote peer may respond with `Piece` or ignore the request.
    Request {
        /// The piece index (0-based).
        index: u32,
        /// Byte offset within the piece where the block starts.
        begin: u32,
        /// Number of bytes to send (typically `BLOCK_SIZE`).
        length: u32,
    },

    /// Piece message (ID = 7, payload = index + begin + data).
    ///
    /// Delivers a block of data in response to a `Request`. The block is
    /// identified by piece index and byte offset; the data follows.
    ///
    /// This is the only message type that transfers actual file content.
    /// The receiver should write the data to the appropriate location and,
    /// once a complete piece is assembled, verify its hash.
    Piece {
        /// The piece index (0-based).
        index: u32,
        /// Byte offset within the piece where this block belongs.
        begin: u32,
        /// The actual block data.
        data: Vec<u8>,
    },

    /// Cancel message (ID = 8, payload = index + begin + length).
    ///
    /// Cancels a previously sent request. The payload is identical to
    /// `Request`: piece index, byte offset, and length.
    ///
    /// Cancellation is primarily used during "endgame mode" when the last
    /// few blocks are requested from multiple peers simultaneously. Once
    /// a block arrives from one peer, cancel requests to others.
    Cancel {
        /// The piece index (0-based).
        index: u32,
        /// Byte offset within the piece.
        begin: u32,
        /// Length of the block that was requested.
        length: u32,
    },
}
@

The [[Message]] enum uses different representations for its variants:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Unit variants} ([[KeepAlive]], [[Choke]], [[Unchoke]], etc.) for
  messages with no payload.

\item \textbf{Tuple variant} ([[Have(u32)]]) for messages with a single simple
  payload.

\item \textbf{Tuple variant with Vec} ([[Bitfield(Vec<u8>)]]) for variable-length
  payloads where the data is opaque.

\item \textbf{Struct variants} ([[Request]], [[Piece]], [[Cancel]]) for messages
  with multiple named fields, improving readability when constructing or
  destructuring these messages.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Message ID Method}

Each message type (except keep-alive) has an associated ID byte. We provide a
method to retrieve this ID, which is essential for serialization and for
protocol handling code that needs to identify message types.

<<message impl>>=
impl Message {
    /// Returns the message type ID, or `None` for keep-alive.
    ///
    /// The message ID is the single byte that identifies the message type
    /// in the wire protocol. Keep-alive messages have no ID because they
    /// have no content at all (length = 0 means no ID byte follows).
    ///
    /// # Returns
    ///
    /// - `Some(id)` for messages with an ID (0-8)
    /// - `None` for `KeepAlive`
    ///
    /// # Example
    ///
    /// ```
    /// use literate_bittorrent::messages::{Message, MSG_CHOKE, MSG_REQUEST};
    ///
    /// assert_eq!(Message::KeepAlive.id(), None);
    /// assert_eq!(Message::Choke.id(), Some(MSG_CHOKE));
    /// assert_eq!(Message::Unchoke.id(), Some(1));
    /// assert_eq!(Message::Interested.id(), Some(2));
    /// assert_eq!(Message::NotInterested.id(), Some(3));
    /// assert_eq!(Message::Have(0).id(), Some(4));
    /// assert_eq!(Message::Bitfield(vec![]).id(), Some(5));
    /// assert_eq!(Message::Request { index: 0, begin: 0, length: 0 }.id(), Some(MSG_REQUEST));
    /// assert_eq!(Message::Piece { index: 0, begin: 0, data: vec![] }.id(), Some(7));
    /// assert_eq!(Message::Cancel { index: 0, begin: 0, length: 0 }.id(), Some(8));
    /// ```
    #[must_use]
    pub fn id(&self) -> Option<u8> {
        match self {
            Message::KeepAlive => None,
            Message::Choke => Some(MSG_CHOKE),
            Message::Unchoke => Some(MSG_UNCHOKE),
            Message::Interested => Some(MSG_INTERESTED),
            Message::NotInterested => Some(MSG_NOT_INTERESTED),
            Message::Have(_) => Some(MSG_HAVE),
            Message::Bitfield(_) => Some(MSG_BITFIELD),
            Message::Request { .. } => Some(MSG_REQUEST),
            Message::Piece { .. } => Some(MSG_PIECE),
            Message::Cancel { .. } => Some(MSG_CANCEL),
        }
    }

    <<message to bytes>>

    <<message from bytes>>
}
@

The [[id()]] method returns [[Option<u8>]] rather than [[u8]] to accurately
represent that keep-alive has no ID. This forces callers to handle the keep-alive
case explicitly, preventing bugs where code assumes all messages have IDs.

\subsubsection{Message Serialization}

To transmit a message over the wire, we must serialize it to the format specified
by BEP 3. Every message (including keep-alive) begins with a 4-byte big-endian
length prefix indicating how many bytes follow. For keep-alive, this length is
zero and no further bytes follow. For all other messages, the length includes
one byte for the message ID plus any payload bytes.

The wire format for each message type is:

\begin{description}
\item[KeepAlive] Length = 0, no ID, no payload.
  Wire format: [[0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00]] (4 bytes total)

\item[Choke, Unchoke, Interested, NotInterested] Length = 1, ID only.
  Wire format: [[0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x01, id]] (5 bytes total)

\item[Have] Length = 5, ID + 4-byte piece index.
  Wire format: [[0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x05, 0x04, index\_be[4]]] (9 bytes total)

\item[Bitfield] Length = 1 + bitfield.len(), ID + raw bitfield bytes.
  Wire format: [[length\_be[4], 0x05, bitfield...]]

\item[Request, Cancel] Length = 13, ID + 4-byte index + 4-byte begin + 4-byte length.
  Wire format: [[0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x0D, id, index\_be[4], begin\_be[4], length\_be[4]]]
  (17 bytes total)

\item[Piece] Length = 9 + data.len(), ID + 4-byte index + 4-byte begin + data.
  Wire format: [[length\_be[4], 0x07, index\_be[4], begin\_be[4], data...]]
\end{description}

All multi-byte integers are encoded in big-endian (network) byte order, as is
standard for network protocols. The length prefix counts only the bytes that
follow---it does not include itself.

<<message to bytes>>=
/// Serializes this message to its wire format.
///
/// The wire format is: `<length><id><payload>` where:
/// - `length` is a 4-byte big-endian integer (bytes following the length field)
/// - `id` is a 1-byte message type identifier (omitted for keep-alive)
/// - `payload` varies by message type
///
/// The returned `Vec<u8>` can be written directly to a TCP socket.
///
/// # Wire Format by Message Type
///
/// | Message | Length | Wire Format |
/// |---------|--------|-------------|
/// | KeepAlive | 0 | `[0,0,0,0]` |
/// | Choke | 1 | `[0,0,0,1,0]` |
/// | Unchoke | 1 | `[0,0,0,1,1]` |
/// | Interested | 1 | `[0,0,0,1,2]` |
/// | NotInterested | 1 | `[0,0,0,1,3]` |
/// | Have | 5 | `[0,0,0,5,4,idx[4]]` |
/// | Bitfield | 1+n | `[len[4],5,bits[n]]` |
/// | Request | 13 | `[0,0,0,13,6,idx[4],begin[4],len[4]]` |
/// | Piece | 9+n | `[len[4],7,idx[4],begin[4],data[n]]` |
/// | Cancel | 13 | `[0,0,0,13,8,idx[4],begin[4],len[4]]` |
///
/// # Example
///
/// ```
/// use literate_bittorrent::messages::Message;
///
/// // Keep-alive is just four zero bytes
/// let keepalive = Message::KeepAlive;
/// assert_eq!(keepalive.to_bytes(), vec![0, 0, 0, 0]);
///
/// // Choke is length=1, id=0
/// let choke = Message::Choke;
/// assert_eq!(choke.to_bytes(), vec![0, 0, 0, 1, 0]);
///
/// // Have is length=5, id=4, then 4-byte piece index
/// let have = Message::Have(42);
/// assert_eq!(have.to_bytes(), vec![0, 0, 0, 5, 4, 0, 0, 0, 42]);
/// ```
#[must_use]
pub fn to_bytes(&self) -> Vec<u8> {
    match self {
        // Keep-alive: length = 0, no ID, no payload
        Message::KeepAlive => vec![0, 0, 0, 0],

        // Messages with no payload: length = 1 (just the ID byte)
        Message::Choke => {
            let mut buf = Vec::with_capacity(5);
            buf.extend_from_slice(&1u32.to_be_bytes()); // length = 1
            buf.push(MSG_CHOKE);
            buf
        }
        Message::Unchoke => {
            let mut buf = Vec::with_capacity(5);
            buf.extend_from_slice(&1u32.to_be_bytes());
            buf.push(MSG_UNCHOKE);
            buf
        }
        Message::Interested => {
            let mut buf = Vec::with_capacity(5);
            buf.extend_from_slice(&1u32.to_be_bytes());
            buf.push(MSG_INTERESTED);
            buf
        }
        Message::NotInterested => {
            let mut buf = Vec::with_capacity(5);
            buf.extend_from_slice(&1u32.to_be_bytes());
            buf.push(MSG_NOT_INTERESTED);
            buf
        }

        // Have: length = 5 (1 byte ID + 4 bytes piece index)
        Message::Have(piece_index) => {
            let mut buf = Vec::with_capacity(9);
            buf.extend_from_slice(&5u32.to_be_bytes()); // length = 5
            buf.push(MSG_HAVE);
            buf.extend_from_slice(&piece_index.to_be_bytes());
            buf
        }

        // Bitfield: length = 1 + bitfield.len()
        Message::Bitfield(bitfield) => {
            let length = 1 + bitfield.len() as u32;
            let mut buf = Vec::with_capacity(4 + 1 + bitfield.len());
            buf.extend_from_slice(&length.to_be_bytes());
            buf.push(MSG_BITFIELD);
            buf.extend_from_slice(bitfield);
            buf
        }

        // Request: length = 13 (1 byte ID + 3 * 4 bytes)
        Message::Request { index, begin, length } => {
            let mut buf = Vec::with_capacity(17);
            buf.extend_from_slice(&13u32.to_be_bytes()); // length = 13
            buf.push(MSG_REQUEST);
            buf.extend_from_slice(&index.to_be_bytes());
            buf.extend_from_slice(&begin.to_be_bytes());
            buf.extend_from_slice(&length.to_be_bytes());
            buf
        }

        // Piece: length = 9 + data.len() (1 byte ID + 4 + 4 + data)
        Message::Piece { index, begin, data } => {
            let length = 9 + data.len() as u32;
            let mut buf = Vec::with_capacity(4 + 9 + data.len());
            buf.extend_from_slice(&length.to_be_bytes());
            buf.push(MSG_PIECE);
            buf.extend_from_slice(&index.to_be_bytes());
            buf.extend_from_slice(&begin.to_be_bytes());
            buf.extend_from_slice(data);
            buf
        }

        // Cancel: length = 13 (same structure as Request)
        Message::Cancel { index, begin, length } => {
            let mut buf = Vec::with_capacity(17);
            buf.extend_from_slice(&13u32.to_be_bytes());
            buf.push(MSG_CANCEL);
            buf.extend_from_slice(&index.to_be_bytes());
            buf.extend_from_slice(&begin.to_be_bytes());
            buf.extend_from_slice(&length.to_be_bytes());
            buf
        }
    }
}
@

The implementation uses [[Vec::with_capacity]] to pre-allocate the exact buffer
size needed, avoiding reallocations. Each message type knows its exact wire
size: fixed-size messages have constant capacities, while variable-size messages
(Bitfield, Piece) compute capacity from their payload length.

The [[to_be_bytes()]] method on [[u32]] returns a [[4; u8]]] array containing
the big-endian representation. We use [[extend_from_slice]] to append these
bytes to the buffer, which is efficient because it copies contiguous memory.

Note that we do not validate the message contents during serialization. For
example, we do not check that a Request's length is a reasonable block size,
or that a Bitfield has valid spare bits. Validation is the responsibility of
higher-level code; the serializer's job is to faithfully encode whatever
message it receives.

