These serialization tests verify:
\begin{itemize}
\item Each message type produces the exact wire format per BEP 3
\item Keep-alive serializes to [[0,0,0,0]] (length=0, no ID)
\item Fixed-length messages have correct constant lengths
\item Variable-length messages compute length correctly
\item Big-endian encoding is correct for all integer fields
\item Serialization is deterministic (same input produces same output)
\item Length prefix matches actual payload size
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Message Parsing (Deserialization)}

When receiving messages from a peer, we must parse bytes from the wire into our
[[Message]] enum. This is the inverse of serialization, but with an important
asymmetry: serialization cannot fail (we control the input), while parsing can
fail in many ways (the peer controls the input).

The wire format reminder:
\begin{description}
\item[All messages] Begin with a 4-byte big-endian length prefix
\item[Keep-alive] Length = 0, no ID, no payload
\item[Other messages] Length includes 1 byte for ID plus payload
\end{description}

The parsing strategy is:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Read the 4-byte length prefix
\item If length is 0, return [[KeepAlive]]
\item Read the message ID byte
\item Based on the ID, validate payload length and parse fields
\item Return appropriate error for invalid input
\end{enumerate}

Each message type has specific payload length requirements:
\begin{itemize}
\item Choke, Unchoke, Interested, NotInterested: payload must be empty (length = 1)
\item Have: payload must be exactly 4 bytes (length = 5)
\item Bitfield: payload can be any length (length >= 1)
\item Request, Cancel: payload must be exactly 12 bytes (length = 13)
\item Piece: payload must be at least 8 bytes for index + begin (length >= 9)
\end{itemize}

<<message from bytes>>=
/// Parses a message from its length-prefixed wire format.
///
/// This method expects a complete message including the 4-byte length prefix.
/// The input must contain exactly the message data---no more, no less.
///
/// # Wire Format
///
/// Messages are structured as `<length><id><payload>` where:
/// - `length` is a 4-byte big-endian integer (bytes after length field)
/// - `id` is a 1-byte message type identifier (omitted for keep-alive)
/// - `payload` varies by message type
///
/// # Arguments
///
/// * `bytes` - Complete message data including the 4-byte length prefix
///
/// # Returns
///
/// * `Ok(Message)` - Successfully parsed message
/// * `Err(PeerError::InvalidMessage)` - Message structure is malformed
/// * `Err(PeerError::UnexpectedMessageId)` - Unknown message type ID
///
/// # Errors
///
/// Returns error if:
/// - Input is too short (less than 4 bytes for length prefix)
/// - Length prefix doesn't match actual data length
/// - Message ID is unknown (not 0-8)
/// - Payload length is invalid for the message type
///
/// # Example
///
/// ```
/// use literate_bittorrent::messages::Message;
///
/// // Parse a keep-alive (length = 0)
/// let keepalive_bytes = vec![0, 0, 0, 0];
/// let msg = Message::from_bytes(&keepalive_bytes).unwrap();
/// assert_eq!(msg, Message::KeepAlive);
///
/// // Parse a choke message (length = 1, id = 0)
/// let choke_bytes = vec![0, 0, 0, 1, 0];
/// let msg = Message::from_bytes(&choke_bytes).unwrap();
/// assert_eq!(msg, Message::Choke);
///
/// // Parse a have message (length = 5, id = 4, piece = 42)
/// let have_bytes = vec![0, 0, 0, 5, 4, 0, 0, 0, 42];
/// let msg = Message::from_bytes(&have_bytes).unwrap();
/// assert_eq!(msg, Message::Have(42));
/// ```
pub fn from_bytes(bytes: &[u8]) -> Result<Self, PeerError> {
    // Minimum: 4 bytes for length prefix
    if bytes.len() < 4 {
        return Err(PeerError::InvalidMessage(format!(
            "message too short: need at least 4 bytes for length prefix, got {}",
            bytes.len()
        )));
    }

    // Extract length prefix (big-endian u32)
    let length = u32::from_be_bytes([bytes[0], bytes[1], bytes[2], bytes[3]]);

    // Verify we have exactly the right amount of data
    let expected_total = 4 + length as usize;
    if bytes.len() != expected_total {
        return Err(PeerError::InvalidMessage(format!(
            "length mismatch: header says {} bytes after prefix, but got {} total bytes (expected {})",
            length,
            bytes.len(),
            expected_total
        )));
    }

    // Keep-alive: length = 0, no ID, no payload
    if length == 0 {
        return Ok(Message::KeepAlive);
    }

    // All other messages have at least an ID byte
    let id = bytes[4];
    let payload = &bytes[5..];
    let payload_len = payload.len();

    match id {
        MSG_CHOKE => {
            if payload_len != 0 {
                return Err(PeerError::InvalidMessage(format!(
                    "choke message must have no payload, got {} bytes",
                    payload_len
                )));
            }
            Ok(Message::Choke)
        }

        MSG_UNCHOKE => {
            if payload_len != 0 {
                return Err(PeerError::InvalidMessage(format!(
                    "unchoke message must have no payload, got {} bytes",
                    payload_len
                )));
            }
            Ok(Message::Unchoke)
        }

        MSG_INTERESTED => {
            if payload_len != 0 {
                return Err(PeerError::InvalidMessage(format!(
                    "interested message must have no payload, got {} bytes",
                    payload_len
                )));
            }
            Ok(Message::Interested)
        }

        MSG_NOT_INTERESTED => {
            if payload_len != 0 {
                return Err(PeerError::InvalidMessage(format!(
                    "not interested message must have no payload, got {} bytes",
                    payload_len
                )));
            }
            Ok(Message::NotInterested)
        }

        MSG_HAVE => {
            if payload_len != 4 {
                return Err(PeerError::InvalidMessage(format!(
                    "have message must have exactly 4 bytes payload, got {}",
                    payload_len
                )));
            }
            let piece_index = u32::from_be_bytes([payload[0], payload[1], payload[2], payload[3]]);
            Ok(Message::Have(piece_index))
        }

        MSG_BITFIELD => {
            // Bitfield can be any length (including empty for peer with no pieces)
            Ok(Message::Bitfield(payload.to_vec()))
        }

        MSG_REQUEST => {
            if payload_len != 12 {
                return Err(PeerError::InvalidMessage(format!(
                    "request message must have exactly 12 bytes payload, got {}",
                    payload_len
                )));
            }
            let index = u32::from_be_bytes([payload[0], payload[1], payload[2], payload[3]]);
            let begin = u32::from_be_bytes([payload[4], payload[5], payload[6], payload[7]]);
            let length = u32::from_be_bytes([payload[8], payload[9], payload[10], payload[11]]);
            Ok(Message::Request { index, begin, length })
        }

        MSG_PIECE => {
            // Piece must have at least index (4) + begin (4) = 8 bytes
            if payload_len < 8 {
                return Err(PeerError::InvalidMessage(format!(
                    "piece message must have at least 8 bytes payload (index + begin), got {}",
                    payload_len
                )));
            }
            let index = u32::from_be_bytes([payload[0], payload[1], payload[2], payload[3]]);
            let begin = u32::from_be_bytes([payload[4], payload[5], payload[6], payload[7]]);
            let data = payload[8..].to_vec();
            Ok(Message::Piece { index, begin, data })
        }

        MSG_CANCEL => {
            if payload_len != 12 {
                return Err(PeerError::InvalidMessage(format!(
                    "cancel message must have exactly 12 bytes payload, got {}",
                    payload_len
                )));
            }
            let index = u32::from_be_bytes([payload[0], payload[1], payload[2], payload[3]]);
            let begin = u32::from_be_bytes([payload[4], payload[5], payload[6], payload[7]]);
            let length = u32::from_be_bytes([payload[8], payload[9], payload[10], payload[11]]);
            Ok(Message::Cancel { index, begin, length })
        }

        unknown => Err(PeerError::UnexpectedMessageId(unknown)),
    }
}
@

The implementation validates inputs at each step:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Length prefix check}: We need at least 4 bytes to read the length.
  Shorter input is immediately rejected with a clear error.

\item \textbf{Data length validation}: The stated length must match the actual
  data. This catches truncated messages and detects buffer misalignment.

\item \textbf{Keep-alive detection}: Length 0 means no ID and no payload.
  We return [[KeepAlive]] immediately without further parsing.

\item \textbf{Message ID dispatch}: We match on the ID byte and parse each
  message type according to its specific requirements.

\item \textbf{Payload length validation}: Each message type validates its
  payload length before parsing. This catches malformed messages early.

\item \textbf{Unknown ID handling}: Any ID outside 0--8 returns
  [[PeerError::UnexpectedMessageId]] with the unknown ID preserved.
\end{enumerate}

Note that we use [[to_vec()]] to copy data into the [[Bitfield]] and [[Piece]]
variants. This ensures the returned [[Message]] owns its data and doesn't
borrow from the input slice, which is important for async code where the
message may outlive the receive buffer.

\subsubsection{Message Parsing Tests}

The parsing tests verify that [[from_bytes]] correctly handles valid messages
and rejects malformed input with appropriate errors.

